          %h4#RelatingStableEquivalencesAndStableWeakHomotopyEquivalences Stable equivalences
          %p
            Here we discuss that the two concepts of
            %em stable equivalences
            and of
            %em stable weak homotopy equivalences
            in def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableEquivalencesForDiagramSpectra"}
            actually agree in the cases of a) pre-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excisive+functors"}> excisive functors
            , b)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+spectra"} orthogonal spectra
            and c)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectra"} sequential spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+spectra"} symmetric spectra
            the class of stable equivalences includes but is strictly larger than that of stable weak homotopy equivalences.
          %p
            This is important in practice, since while the stable equivalences are the weak equivalences in the stable model structure of theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableModelStructuresOnDiagramSpectra"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+weak+homotopy+equivalences"} stable weak homotopy equivalences
            that are typically more readily identified.
          #RelationBetweenStableEquivalencesAndStableWeakHomotopyEquivalencesForDiagramSpectra.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕊
                      %mi Mod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}Mod
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕊
                      %mi Orth
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}_{Orth} Mod
              and in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕊
                      %mi Seq
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}_{Seq} Mod
              we have for the concepts from def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableEquivalencesForDiagramSpectra"}
              that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi stable
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi weak
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi homotopy
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi equivalence
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi stable
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi equivalence
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    stable\;weak\;homotopy\;equivalence
                    \\;\Leftrightarrow\;
                    stable \; equivalence
                    \\,.
            %p
              In
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕊
                      %mi Sym
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}_{Sym}Mod
              however we only have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi stable
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi weak
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi homotopy
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi equivalence
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi stable
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi equivalence
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    stable\;weak\;homotopy\;equivalence
                    \\;\Rightarrow\;
                    stable \; equivalence
            %p but the reverse implication is false.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#MMSS00"}> MMSS00, prop. 8.7, prop. 8.8
            )
          %p
            We break up this statement below as prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableWeakHomotopyEquivalenceIsStableEquivalence"}
            and prop.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableEquivalencesThatAreStableWeakHomotopyEquivalences"}
          %p
            The argument that every stable weak homotopy equivalence is in particular a stable equivalence is fairly formal; this we turn to first in prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableWeakHomotopyEquivalenceIsStableEquivalence"}
            below. The converse statement in prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableEquivalencesThatAreStableWeakHomotopyEquivalences"}
            however relies on explicit analysis of the class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            of generating acylic cofibrations in def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GeneratingAndGeneratingAcyclicCofibrationsForDiagramSpectra"}
          #AKindOfAlmostSpectrification.num_defn
            %h6#definition_10 Definition
            %p
